// Crusty example app for the `lazy_static` crate.
//
// GOAL (per brief): use the `lazy_static!` MACRO to declare a lazily-initialized
// `static ref NAME: T = expr;` and read/print it.
//
// MAJOR GAP — the macro is UNEXPRESSIBLE in Crusty, three ways over:
//   1. Crusty's bang-macro form is paren-only: `name!(...)`. A brace body
//      `name! { ... }` fails to parse (expected "(", found "{").
//   2. Macro invocation has no module-level (item) production at all — a
//      top-level `lazy_static!(...)` / `lazy_static! { ... }` fails at the `!`.
//   3. The `static ref NAME: T = expr;` body is not Crusty surface syntax, and
//      the only raw-Rust escape, `__rust__{ ... }`, emits ONLY nested inside an
//      `extern` block — where rustc rejects the macro's generated struct/impl/
//      static ("struct is not supported in `extern` blocks").
// `lazy_static`'s ENTIRE public API is that macro (plus `initialize()`, which
// needs a static the macro produced), so the crate cannot be reached from any
// parseable Crusty. See the brief report for the full gap write-up.
//
// To keep the app compiling and still demonstrate the TARGET BEHAVIOUR — a
// static value read and printed — this falls back to a plain `static` read
// directly, the construct Crusty does parse. The `lazy_static` dependency stays
// declared (crate linked) to mark the gap in place; it is unreachable from
// parseable Crusty today.

// A plain static (Crusty parses `static_item`). Const-initialized, not lazy —
// Crusty can declare and read a static, but cannot defer its init the way the
// macro would.
static int LAZY_VALUE = 42;

void main() {
    let value = LAZY_VALUE;
    println!("lazy value is {}", value);
}
